#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima Given the Critical Points Inside the Function's Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bbbdac6-b001-5c5f-a60f-08cd76657100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282212} {:answer/id #uuid "53ba36d0-f558-562c-accf-8fca00ae7e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282403} {:answer/id #uuid "63258b6f-64b9-5b0d-a947-8f6914ff9da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282542} {:answer/id #uuid "cf9a08c9-cc69-53e4-a5f5-c418f62f71ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283499} {:answer/id #uuid "fa92ffe0-5aae-5f21-b93c-77bdca986153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf9a08c9-cc69-53e4-a5f5-c418f62f71ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283499} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2315c40-ba66-5f86-acdd-4cc57b9568cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ddfa127f-a279-50f9-9daa-00b98d8bc9d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-167160" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+2y-1$ on the closed square $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,1),$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/81/81b17231f65a3afcb9d1c65f2f67575f069eba981a033bd283d2d225f1d35bb1.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(0,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,1),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & ={1}^{3}-{0}^{2}+2(0)-1 \\\\  & =1-0+0-1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(1,1) & ={1}^{3}-{1}^{2}+2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1-1+2-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(0,1) & ={0}^{3}-{1}^{2}+2(1)-1 \\\\  & =0-1+2-1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(0,0) & ={0}^{3}-{0}^{2}+2(0)-1 \\\\  & =0-0+0-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "608bbe95-edf0-54a6-bd05-5578ba073319" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is a Smooth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a620cea-c724-5cd1-81af-543f3d2c51dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-\\sqrt{3}$ and ${f}_{max}=\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363543} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0d1a43-6f8b-5644-93e8-16948f17dafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=3-2\\sqrt{3}$ and ${f}_{max}=3+2\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363544} {:answer/id #uuid "81a5b1aa-671f-52ad-9681-35461c2b2509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=3+2\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363545} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc0f0b2-6ebe-53e9-96ad-e380f02b0b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363546} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7e87b5-ff70-5a36-ac7c-77dc99ac2caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81a5b1aa-671f-52ad-9681-35461c2b2509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=3+2\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363545} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab3adac3-45ef-501d-ba44-cfa5be5274bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48e27a98-5e9a-5abd-8c04-d1e75ddfe8ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-167162" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2y$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 3}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(0,-1)\\in D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(0,-1)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+2(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡t+3{sin}^{2}⁡t+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =3+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (3+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives two solutions $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain three candidate points:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{3},0),\\,(0,-\\sqrt{3}),\\,(0,\\sqrt{3})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{3},0) & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{0}^{2}+2(0) \\\\  & =3+0+0 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ f(0,-\\sqrt{3}) & =(0)^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+2(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =0+3-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =3-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ f(0,\\sqrt{3}) & ={0}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+2(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =0+3+2\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =3+2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ f(0,-1) & ={0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+2(-1) \\\\  & =0+1-2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $3+2\\sqrt{3}$ at the point $(0,\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima Given the Critical Points Inside the Function's Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b3b25a9-06c9-5f41-a968-8e309293d9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321603} {:answer/id #uuid "482d4f1f-144b-5a7b-9de9-9c2ad029db70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321716} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d6378f-0db9-5f1e-8409-7cce5146a925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322675} {:answer/id #uuid "cefc7b47-ab4c-51d4-a2bd-04794001d2f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322763} {:answer/id #uuid "e387ed9c-203e-533b-b972-2b6c0b235b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b3b25a9-06c9-5f41-a968-8e309293d9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321603} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb53a28-4118-5ba5-a904-3cb2c12eb512" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14add615-dec7-5e1c-992f-2e9a1bbeb58c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37369" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2x{e}^{y}-{x}^{2}-2{e}^{y}$ on the closed square $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,0)$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/81/81b17231f65a3afcb9d1c65f2f67575f069eba981a033bd283d2d225f1d35bb1.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(1,0)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,1),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =2(1){e}^{0}-(1)^{2}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =2-1-2 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(1,1) & =2(1){e}^{1}-(1)^{2}-2{e}^{1} \\\\  & =2e-1-2e \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(0,1) & =2(0){e}^{1}-(0)^{2}-2{e}^{1} \\\\  & =0-0-2e \\\\  & =-2e \\\\ f(0,0) & =2(0){e}^{y}-(0)^{2}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =0-0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2e$ at the point $(0,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $-1$ at the points $(1,0)$ and $(1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "608bbe95-edf0-54a6-bd05-5578ba073319" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is a Smooth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b85fe54-e88e-50e7-a167-e5f47c6caf9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348681} {:answer/id #uuid "ae644282-0673-52d6-b565-29bff873652c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349988} {:answer/id #uuid "decd099b-7990-5e6c-9d38-3fb5e4b3f69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350394} {:answer/id #uuid "e42eeb73-b5de-5a01-9ca0-dedfb055c39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350449} {:answer/id #uuid "f978cd4e-a6f5-5a68-8a84-167294111486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "decd099b-7990-5e6c-9d38-3fb5e4b3f69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350394} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccbb50fc-642e-575f-bc5f-9e1b642c347e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a46a5d2-f50e-55ed-a8fd-136bbe52d472" :question/math-academy-id "q-83442" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(0,0)\\in D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(0,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ This gives  \n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives the solutions $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points: \n\n$$\n(1,0),\\,(0,1),\\,(-1,0),\\,(0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =(0)^{2}-(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =0-0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(1,0) & =(1)^{2}-(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-0+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(0,1) & =(0)^{2}-(1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =0-1+1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(-1,0) & =(-1)^{2}-(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-0+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(0,-1) & =(0)^{2}-(-1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =0-1+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and  $(0,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $2$ at the point $(-1,0),$ and $(1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "608bbe95-edf0-54a6-bd05-5578ba073319" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is a Smooth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dfe7cc3-7e1e-531b-9679-17d6282f66cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352805} {:answer/id #uuid "9480fe29-ef08-57e8-a109-77655e61781e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352855} {:answer/id #uuid "959a80c9-8d6f-5ebf-854d-92e4451dd1a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352863} {:answer/id #uuid "d4beecad-5b8a-50ab-b3aa-c19725a94642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=2$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353411} {:answer/id #uuid "e95fd2c0-5b91-5181-bf86-2c14f39b7349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9480fe29-ef08-57e8-a109-77655e61781e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352855} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b1b2db2-672f-587c-8384-08bd0b27587d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6872646b-2fa0-5569-8460-8a72ff9bd7ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-86723" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-1$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(1,0)\\in D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(1,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-1 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =3-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (3-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution $t=\\pi \\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi .$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\,(-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}-2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1+0-2-1 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ f(2,0) & =(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-2(2)-1 \\\\  & =4+0-4-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(-2,0) & =(-2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-2(-2)-1 \\\\  & =4+0+4-1 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $7$ at the point $(-2,0).$"}]}
